## Open the PORTS tab on the bottom of your IDE and locate the URLs for the web UIs of the Python and Java frontends: Python/Flask frontend - the service with port 5001 (http://localhost:5001), Java/Thymeleaf frontend - the service with port 8090 (http://localhost:8090)

## Start by opening the Python frontend in your browser on URL http://localhost:5001

## You can feel free to add some “ToDo” items yourself and/or set some of them as done. Most likely you will also see an item called “Sample” come and go. This item is being set and removed by a load generator.

## Repeat the same steps with the alternate frontend running on http://localhost:8090.

## property by which components identify themselves within the OpenTelemetry scope.
## backend-springboot
environment:
- OTEL_RESOURCE_ATTRIBUTES=service.name=todobackend-springboot

## frontend-flask
environment:
- OTEL_RESOURCE_ATTRIBUTES=service.name=frontend-flask

## frontend-thymeleaf
environment:
- OTEL_RESOURCE_ATTRIBUTES=service.name=frontend-thymeleaf

## shared config
environment:
- OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_ENDPOINT=${OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_ENDPOINT}

## The actual value of this endpoint is specified in an .env file
OTEL_COLLECTOR_HOST=otelcol
OTEL_COLLECTOR_PORT_GRPC=4317
OTEL_COLLECTOR_PORT_HTTP=4318
OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_ENDPOINT=http://${OTEL_COLLECTOR_HOST}:${OTEL_COLLECTOR_PORT_GRPC}

## Distributed Tracing using Jaeger UI
## You can access the Jaeger web UI at the following link: http://localhost:16686/
## Services”.it will show all the services for which Jaeger has tracing information.
## On the list of traces, identify one that is called todoui-flask: /add
## Click on the item that says “10 Spans” for further analysis.
## select the second span in the hierarchy, which is called todoui-flask POST
## Once clicked, a nested summary of details is shown directly beneath the span. If you click on the drop-down arrows to the left of Tags and Processes more span details are visible.
##  Select the trace labeled todobackend-springboot TodoRepository.save. You will see the details of the span that are provided by the implementation of the OpenTelemetry agent. 
## In order to simulate a decrease in performance, you can add an item called slow.
## Open the Python or Java frontend and submit an item named slow.
## Besides the slow running component you can also simulate a failure in this lab. If you enter fail instead of slow, it will cause a RuntimeException on the backend component.
## switch back to the Jaeger UI and see how this failure is being displayed. Recent trace is highlighted in red.
# Go back to http://localhost:16686/, Expand “Services", select the todoui-flask: / and the todoui-thymeleaf: GET /, Click on “Compare Traces”. A new window will appear displaying the traces in a visual flow next to each other. This window also shows a different collection of spans for the Java and Python part to the left and an identical one for the backend on the right.


## Observing Metrics with Prometheus
## Go to http://localhost:9090/, To get a list of all the metrics that are currently available, click on the metrics explorer icon
## Or, if you type in otel or jvm, you can see that there is a range of metrics coming from the OpenTelemetry collector itself.
## By selecting the tab “Graph” instead of “Table”, the data is visualized in a graphical form. You can adjust the time range as well.

